In `LoggerWithConfig`, a nil `config.Output` is never left nil: the defaults block sets it to `DefaultLoggerConfig.Output` (middleware/logger.go:95-97), and since `DefaultLoggerConfig` doesn't set `Output`, it stays nil. So at write time the fallback branch runs (lines 215-218):

    if config.Output == nil {
        _, err = c.Logger().Output().Write(buf.Bytes())
        return
    }

i.e. the log line is written to the current request's Echo logger output, `c.Logger().Output()` — the `echo.Logger` of the `Echo` instance (default `log.New("echo")`, whose output is `os.Stdout`), not to the middleware's own config output. That matches the doc comment "Optional. Default value os.Stdout."

It returns `echo.NewHTTPError(http.StatusInternalServerError, i.(error).Error())` — i.e. a 500 HTTP error whose message is the `error` value that the pool's `New` function produced (the pool's `New` returns an `error` when `gzip.NewWriterLevel`/`gzip.NewReader` fails). The code is:

    i := pool.Get()
    gr, ok := i.(*gzip.Reader)
    if !ok {
        return echo.NewHTTPError(http.StatusInternalServerError, i.(error).Error())
    }

Note it asserts `i.(error)` there, so a pool item that is neither `*gzip.Reader` nor `error` would panic — but for the default pool the only non-reader value is the error from `New`.

It returns the number of bytes just read together with `echo.ErrStatusRequestEntityTooLarge` (a 413 HTTPError) as the error:

    func (r *limitedReader) Read(b []byte) (n int, err error) {
        n, err = r.reader.Read(b)
        r.read += int64(n)
        if r.read > r.limit {
            return n, echo.ErrStatusRequestEntityTooLarge
        }
        return
    }

So once the cumulative `r.read` exceeds `config.limit`, every further read returns `(n, echo.ErrStatusRequestEntityTooLarge)`; the underlying read error is discarded in that case.

`DefaultBodyDumpConfig.Skipper` is `DefaultSkipper` (middleware/body_dump.go:36-38), i.e. the function in middleware/middleware.go:

    // DefaultSkipper returns false which processes the middleware.
    func DefaultSkipper(echo.Context) bool {
        return false
    }

So by default no request is skipped — the BodyDump middleware always runs.
